```ADD_PAPER
2203.01143v2
This paper develops simulation models and decision‐making protocols for multi‐stage screening pipelines under uncertainty. Its simulation studies characterize screening performance by quantifying how the covariance structure across pipeline stages can impact overall outcomes, including cases where screening can perform worse than random selection. The extensive experiments provide detailed performance benchmarks, with scenarios varying key parameters (such as screening specificity, cost trade-offs, and stage-wise uncertainty) that reveal how much value is gained through improved prediction versus expanded screening access. These experimental results validate that optimizing a decision‐making framework benefits from a careful balance between predictive accuracy and screening coverage—concepts that translate naturally into a prediction-access ratio (PAR). The methodologies and simulation protocols presented here offer a promising foundation for reproducing theoretical analyses and can be extended to case studies like the German long‐term unemployment example by adapting the framework to quantify the marginal benefit of improved prediction relative to broader screening access for identifying the worst-off.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
