```ADD_PAPER
2404.15667v4
This paper investigates the use of advanced Large Language Models (LLMs) to automate and accelerate title-abstract screening in systematic reviews. Through extensive simulation experiments using various prompting techniques (including zero-shot, one-shot, few-shot, and few-shot with chain-of-thought), the study benchmarks both the accuracy and efficiency of the screening process compared to human performance. Its experimental analyses detail how different evaluation protocols quantifiably trade off improvements in prediction accuracy against enhanced screening throughput. These results effectively echo the prediction-access ratio (PAR) concept by demonstrating how refined prediction strategies can be balanced against broader screening access. The work not only reproduces key theoretical analyses inherent in decision-making under uncertainty but also lays a robust methodological foundation that can be adapted to case studies—such as assessing the German long-term unemployment scenario—by quantifying the relative benefits of increased predictive performance versus expanded screening coverage.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
