```ADD_PAPER
2403.13536v1
This study presents an integrated framework that pairs machine learning‐driven unemployment prediction with simulation‐based evaluation protocols to quantify the relative benefits of refining predictive accuracy versus broadening population screening—formalized as the prediction‐access ratio (PAR). Uniquely, the paper reproduces its theoretical analyses through detailed numerical experiments and applies the methodology to a German long‐term unemployment case study. Its simulation results are extensively discussed: they reveal that while improvements in prediction accuracy can substantially lower aggregate unemployment estimates, expanding screening to reach more individuals yields broader coverage of at‐risk subgroups, especially among the worst‐off. The experimental analyses dissect various policy parameters, illustrating how adjustments in model precision and screening volume interact in non-linear ways, thus offering policymakers a robust decision‐making tool for targeted interventions. This work represents a significant stride toward harmonizing statistical rigor with real-world socio-economic challenges.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
