2507.00001v1
This work introduces a comprehensive simulation framework to rigorously quantify the trade-offs between refining prediction accuracy and expanding screening access, a balance captured by the prediction‐access ratio (PAR). The study designs a suite of modeling and evaluation protocols—incorporating Monte Carlo simulations, sensitivity analyses, and robust optimization methods—to systematically analyze how incremental improvements in model precision compare to broader screening coverage when identifying high-risk, worst‐off subpopulations. Extensive experimental results on both synthetic datasets and a replicated German long‐term unemployment case study reveal non-linear effects: while small improvements in predictive performance can considerably reduce false negatives in a high-precision regime, increasing screening access dramatically expands the identification of at-risk individuals under uncertainty. These novel simulation insights offer policymakers a clear, quantitative basis for allocating resources between enhancing predictive diagnostics and broadening the screening process.
``` 
