This paper introduces an innovative simulation platform that quantitatively assesses the trade-offs between increasing predictive accuracy and enlarging screening access for identifying high-risk, worst-off subpopulations. The authors define a novel metric, the prediction‐access ratio (PAR), which is derived from a suite of modeling and evaluation protocols that include agent‐based simulations, Monte Carlo experiments, and sensitivity analyses specifically applied to the German long‐term unemployment case study. Detailed experimental evaluations reveal that modest enhancements in predictive precision may be surpassed in overall impact by expanding screening reach, particularly in high-uncertainty scenarios where non-linear threshold effects emerge. The study also reproduces its theoretical analyses with extensive statistical breakdowns, highlighting how resource allocation can be streamlined to optimize the balance between model refinement and expanded access. In doing so, it provides policymakers with actionable insights for efficiently targeting interventions among the most vulnerable groups.
