Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: Quantifying the Prediction–Access Trade-off for Identifying the Worst-Off: A Dual-Track Simulation and Empirical Study

Overview:
Our goal is to design a streamlined experiment that both reproduces and extends previous work on the prediction‐access ratio (PAR). We will pursue two parallel tracks:
 • Track A – Theory-Driven Simulation: Under controlled Gaussian and log‐normal assumptions, we will simulate screening outcomes to compute V(α, f; β) and derive PAR.
 • Track B – Empirical Case Study: Using a simplified version of the German long‐term unemployment administrative records (or synthetic data calibrated to these records), we will demonstrate how resource allocation decisions change in practice.

Key Steps:
1. Theoretical Simulation (Track A)
 a. Synthetic Data Generation:
  • Generate outcome Y from Gaussian and log‐normal distributions.
  • Define a “risk score” f (e.g., a noisy linear predictor) with variable R² (ranging from near-random, R² → 0, to near-perfect, R² → 1).
 b. Screening Protocol:
  • Define the screening capacity parameter α (grid: 0.01, 0.02, …, 0.50) and set welfare threshold t(β) based on β-quantiles (β ∈ {0.05, 0.15, 0.25}).
  • Operate under a monotone screening rule: individuals with f above a threshold are screened.
 c. Simulation Model & Evaluation:
  • Use Monte Carlo simulations to compute the policy value V(α, f; β) = Pr[screened | Y ≤ t(β)].
  • Vary grid parameters for α, β, and R² as well as finite improvements Δα and ΔR² (choose Δ ∈ {0.01, 0.05, 0.10}).
  • Compute the Prediction–Access Ratio (PAR) as the relative gain in V achieved per unit gain in screening access (Δα) versus per unit gain in prediction quality (ΔR²). Capture both local (infinitesimal) and finite improvement regimes.
 d. Visualization:
  • Reproduce key figures: (i) a screening policy surface, (ii) PAR surfaces over the α-R² grid, (iii) plots of PAR vs. α for fixed R², and (iv) local improvement curves.
  • Optionally include cost sensitivity analysis using a marginal cost ratio (C_access/C_pred) to indicate when expanding access is more cost-effective than improving prediction.

2. Empirical Case Study (Track B)
 a. Data/Model:
  • Secure a simplified version of German long‐term unemployment administrative records or generate realistic synthetic data calibrated to such records.
  • Implement a straightforward machine learning model (e.g., elastic net regression) to predict unemployment outcomes.
 b. Deployment Protocol:
  • Apply the same screening metric V(α, f; β) from Track A to this dataset.
  • Adjust the prediction model to simulate varying R² regimes by adding controlled noise.
 c. Analysis:
  • Compare improvements in predictive accuracy versus expanded screening access over policy-relevant parameter grids.
  • Generate figures analogous to those in Track A to illustrate the trade-offs and derive actionable insights in a real-world context.
  • Optionally, conduct subgroup analyses (e.g., demographics, regions) to assess fairness and robustness.

Implementation Notes:
 • Use Python with libraries such as NumPy, SciPy for simulation, scikit-learn (for regression models), and matplotlib/seaborn for visualization.
 • Ensure replicability by clearly documenting assumptions (e.g., the stability of distributional parameters, monotonicity of the screening rule) and deviations from theory.
 • Emphasize the experiment’s simplicity: focus on one simulation cycle with representative parameter grids rather than exhaustive exploration, ensuring the novelty lies in operationalizing the PAR metric and balancing two public policy interventions.

Conclusion:
This two-track plan integrates rigorous simulation modeling with a real-world case study, using both theory-driven and data-driven machine learning models. It innovatively quantifies the trade-off between enhancing prediction quality and expanding screening access, yielding clear visual and quantitative benchmarks for policy decision-making regarding the identification of the worst-off populations.

Next Steps:
• Begin with Track A to solidify simulation parameters and verify the behavior of PAR under controlled conditions.
• Proceed to Track B using available (or synthetic) unemployment data to validate findings in a policy-relevant setting.
• Prepare comprehensive documentation and visualizations to guide future extensions, such as fairness diagnostics and cost-adjusted recommendations.

This plan offers a clear and innovative experimental setup to push forward the methodology of balancing prediction and access in resource-limited screening settings.
                    
 
