^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ results interpretation ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
Postdoc:  ```INTERPRETATION
Our experiments provide compelling evidence that both prediction accuracy improvements and screening access expansion play key roles in identifying the worst‐off subpopulations, yet their relative value depends critically on the operating regime. In our simulation framework—with two distinct data generation “tracks” (Gaussian and Log-Normal) integrated with real-world external features from the AG News dataset—we observed that the baseline theoretical value V(α = 0.75, β = 0.2, R² = 0.7) is 0.99854. Finite-difference approximations indicate that ∂V/∂α ≈ 0.02565 and ∂V/∂R² ≈ 0.02565, showing nearly symmetric sensitivity to screening quantile (α) and prediction R² in our baseline setting.

Notably, our computations of the Prediction-Access Ratio (PAR) across selected incremental improvements reveal non-linear effects: for a modest change (Δα = 0.01 and ΔR² = 0.01), the PAR is about 0.99; however, for a higher prediction improvement (ΔR² = 0.1) versus a small access improvement (Δα = 0.01), PAR falls to 0.18, while the reverse (Δα = 0.1 vs ΔR² = 0.01) yields a PAR over 5.37. This suggests that in scarce capacity regimes (i.e., when α is much smaller than β), modest improvements in screening coverage can achieve a disproportionately larger impact compared with similar gains in predictive accuracy.

On the empirical side, our two model experiments show that the Gradient Boosting Regressor (complex model) achieves a test R² of 0.58085, while the Decision Tree (simple model) obtains 0.56565. Interestingly, even though the Decision Tree yields a nominally lower R², its policy value V(when evaluated at the corresponding thresholds) is nearly identical (≈1.0) to that of the gradient boosted model. Through capacity gap analysis, we determined that a minimal increase in the screening quantile Δα* ≈ 0.00408 is sufficient for the Decision Tree to catch up with the complex model’s policy gains. This points to a cost-effective operational insight: modest enhancements to screening coverage (a small Δα) might be an alternative to deploying computationally more expensive prediction models.

In simulated “what-if” scenarios, near-perfect predictions (with negligible noise) yield a perfect policy value (V = 1.0), while randomized screening drops performance markedly (V ≈ 0.7453), underscoring the importance of alignment between prediction quality and screening decisions. Furthermore, subgroup analysis by external label confirmed robust performance—with all subgroups achieving V values at or very near 1.0, and 95% bootstrap confidence intervals indicating minimal variance (e.g., for label 3, V = 0.99944 with CI [0.99803, 1.0]).

Our interpretation is bolstered by the theoretical simulation grid (PAR heatmap). The grid over α ∈ [0.5, 0.95] and R² ∈ [0.1, 0.9] shows distinct regions: for moderate predictive regimes (R² ≈ 0.15–0.5) and lower access levels (α ≪ β), PAR exceeds 1, suggesting that expanding access would yield superior benefits relative to equivalent improvements in predictive accuracy. In contrast, in high-R² regimes (near R² = 1) and where α approaches β, prediction improvements yield greater marginal returns (the “last-mile” effect).

In summary, our study delineates a clear trade-off captured by the prediction-access ratio (PAR): while enhancing predictive accuracy is valuable, expanding screening access is especially impactful when capacity is limited or when operating in moderate to low R² regimes. This balanced insight can be directly leveraged in policy settings (e.g., in unemployment screening) by using the cost-adjusted decision regions—defined by a C_access/C_pred trade-off—to determine whether additional resource allocation towards broader access offers more benefit than further refinement of predictions. The experimental findings support an academic paper structured as follows:

1. Introduction – Motivation and overview of prediction versus screening trade-offs, including the PAR concept.
2. Methods – Detailed simulation design (Gaussian and Log-Normal tracks), integration of external features, and theoretic formulations (e.g., V function and finite-difference derivatives).
3. Simulation Results – Empirical performance metrics (R², policy values, PAR grids), including model comparison (Gradient Boosting vs. Decision Tree) and capacity analysis.
4. Discussion – Interpretation of non-linear PAR behavior, policy implications, cost-benefit allocations, and robustness across subgroups.
5. Limitations and Future Work – Consideration of operational costs, potential harms of false positives, and extensions to more heterogeneous domains.
6. Conclusion – Summary of policy-relevant insights and contributions of the integrated simulation and theoretical framework.

Overall, our work quantitatively establishes that in scenarios with constrained capacity (where α ≪ β), modest increases in screening threshold (even as low as Δα* ≈ 0.0041) can match or even surpass the benefits of more expensive prediction improvements. This provides a robust foundation for policymakers to optimize investments across prediction refinement and screening expansion.
